{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791044134,"nonce":"IMHweHATTosnjZzNZ9y04wQ8Bao=","title":"The SDF shaders are twelve hand-maintained copies of one body of code","message":"Measured across `crates/blinc_gpu/src/shaders/sdf_*.wgsl`: 50 function\nbodies appear in more than one file, about 164 KiB of 547 KiB of source.\n`sd_rounded_rect`, `sd_circle` and `sd_ellipse` exist twelve times each;\n`sd_shaped_rect`, `superellipse_dist`, `erf` and `shadow_rounded_rect`\nnine times each; `calculate_polygon_clip_alpha` eight.\n\nThe cost is already paid twice. 2ad76a9 had to be applied to nine\nidentical copies of `sd_shaped_rect`. 178a63b, the clip-path fill rule,\nneeds twelve edits to one function before it can ship.\n\n\nThe downlevel variants looked like a second implementation. For\n`calculate_polygon_clip_alpha` the entire difference is two lines:\n\n    -   let data = aux_data[vec_idx];\n    +   let data = load_aux(vec_idx);\n\nAn accessor swap, not different logic. If the shared helpers read aux\ndata through a `load_aux` indirection that each family defines — direct\nindexing in the normal family, the existing shim in `_dt` — the helpers\nbecome identical across all twelve and the two-variant split collapses.\nWorth checking whether that holds for the other divergent pairs.\n\n\n- A `build.rs` concatenating fragments from `shaders/common/` ahead of\n  each body, writing composed sources to `OUT_DIR` for `include_str!`.\n  No new dependency, and naga still validates the composed output.\n- `naga_oil`, which adds a real `#import` to WGSL and is what Bevy uses.\n  A new dependency, and it changes the authoring format.\n- Runtime concatenation in Rust. Simplest, but then no file is\n  independently validatable by `naga \u003cfile\u003e`, which CI does today.\n\nTwo things to get right either way. Shader error line numbers shift once\nanything is prepended, so the common block wants to be first and stable.\nAnd CI's `naga` step and the per-shader parse tests should point at the\ncomposed output — which is an improvement, since today they validate\ntext that is not quite what reaches the GPU.\n\n\nRaised as an analogue to ashui/hwgpu's HXSL, which builds shaders with\nHaxe macros. A Rust proc macro can parse a DSL and emit WGSL, so the\nshape ports. What does not port is the part that makes HXSL pleasant:\nHaxe macros see resolved type information and can reflect on the\napplication's types, while a Rust proc macro sees only tokens and cannot\nresolve an alias or look into another crate.\n\nFor flat POD shader structs that limitation does not bite, which is why\nthe struct-mirroring case below is a good fit and a full typed DSL is\nnot. `rust-gpu` writes shaders in Rust proper and is a much larger\nchange, needing SPIR-V ingestion.","files":null}]}